A '''knowledge market''' is an arrangement through which people or organizations exchange access to information, expertise, research or other knowledge-related resources. Exchange may involve money, reputation, reciprocal assistance or institutional incentives.

== Economic characteristics ==
Knowledge-related goods may have high creation costs and low marginal copying costs. Access can be restricted by contracts, intellectual-property rights or technology even when copying itself is inexpensive. A buyer may have difficulty judging an answer before acquiring it, while a seller may not be able to fully disclose its value without transferring it. Asymmetric information, search costs and non-rivalry complicate straightforward commodity-market analogies.

== Coordination mechanisms ==
Hayek's analysis of dispersed knowledge explains why coordination must make use of local information. A knowledge market may use price, reputation, peer evaluation, matching and intermediaries. For an illustrative exchange, trade is feasible when the buyer's expected value minus search and verification cost exceeds the seller's reservation cost, with a mutually acceptable price. This simple model omits strategic behavior, uncertain quality and benefits to third parties. Reputation systems can help but are vulnerable to collusion or popularity bias.

== Measurement and design ==
Evaluate match quality using externally scored answers, repeat demand, resolution time and access inequality. Separate seller incentives from evidence standards; pay for a verified outcome or review rather than merely for confident prose where appropriate. Knowledge commons and open science may use non-price coordination and are not reducible to markets. A functioning exchange mechanism is not a guarantee of truth, fair access or social welfare.

